WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:11.090
In 2019, researchers at OpenAI came up with
an amazing learning algorithm that they deployed

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:16.650
on a robot hand that was able to dexterously
manipulate a Rubik’s cube…even when it

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:22.130
was severely hamstrung. A good game plan to
perform such a thing, is to first, solve the

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:28.599
problem in a computer simulation where we
can learn and iterate quickly, and then, transfer

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:33.719
everything the agent learned there to the
real world, and hope that it obtained general

00:00:33.719 --> 00:00:40.940
knowledge that indeed can be applied to real
tasks. Papers like these are some of my favorites.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.920
If you are one of our core Fellow Scholars,
you may also remember that we talked about

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:51.570
walking robots about 200 episodes ago. In
this amazing paper, we witnessed a robot not

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:57.800
only learning to walk, but, it could also
adjust its behavior and keep walking, even

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:04.320
if one or multiple legs lose power, or get
damaged. In this previous work, the key idea

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:11.320
was to allow the robot to learn tasks such
as walking not only in one, optimal way, but

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:17.970
to explore and build a map of many alternative
motions relying on different body parts.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:23.790
Both of these papers teach us that working
in the real world often shows us new, unexpected

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:30.119
challenges to overcome. And, this new paper
offers a technique to adapt a robot arm to

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:35.110
these challenges after it has been deployed
into the real world. It is supposed to be

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:40.890
able to pick up objects, which sounds somewhat
simple these days…until we realize that

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:47.090
new, previously unseen objects may appear
in the bin with different shapes, or material

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:52.900
models. For example, reflective and refractive
objects are particularly perilous because

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.509
they often show us more about their surroundings
than about themselves, lighting conditions

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:05.000
may also change after deployment, the gripper’s
length or shape may change, and many, many

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.220
other issues are likely to arise.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:12.580
Let’s have a look at the lighting conditions
part. Why would that be such an issue? The

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:18.980
objects are the same, the scene looks nearly
the same, so, why is this a challenge? Well,

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:25.190
if the lighting changes, the reflections change
significantly, and since the robot arm sees

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:32.239
its reflection and thinks that it is a different
object, it just keeps trying to grasp it.

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:37.340
After some fine-tuning, this method was able
to increase the otherwise not too pleasant

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:46.330
32% success rate to 63%. Much, much better.
Also, extending the gripper used to be somewhat

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:52.349
of a problem, but as you see here, with this
technique, it is barely an issue anymore.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:58.860
Also, if we have a somewhat intelligent system,
and we move position of the gripper around,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:05.760
nothing really changes, so we would expect
it to perform well. Does it? Well, let’s

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.440
have a look! Unfortunately, it just seems
to be rotating around without too many meaningful

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.830
actions. And now, hold on to your papers,
because after using this continual learning

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:23.280
scheme, yes! It improved substantially and
makes very few mistakes, and can even pick

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.849
up these tiny objects that are very challenging
to grasp with this clumsy hand.

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:35.599
This fine-tuning step typically takes an additional
hour, or at most a few hours of extra training,

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:42.129
and can used to help these AIs learn continuously
after they are deployed in the real world,

00:03:42.129 --> 00:03:47.620
thereby updating and improving themselves.
It is hard to define what exactly intelligence

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:54.090
is, but an important component of it is being
able to reuse knowledge and adapt to new,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:04:00.410
unseen situations. This is exactly what this
paper helps with. Absolute witchcraft. What

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:02.110
a time to be alive!

